/// Generalized shafts as stacks of coaxial segments along +Z: diameters and
/// tapers so far, joined so that no surface lies inside the solid where one
/// segment meets the next.

// Shaft: generalized shafts
//
// A shaft is a stack of segments on the +Z axis, each placed by the two planes
// it runs between, measured from one datum the way a baseline-dimensioned
// drawing gives them:
//
//   shaft.body([shaft.diameter(20.0, 0.0, 20.0),
//               shaft.taper(20.0, 12.0, 20.0, 26.0),
//               shaft.diameter(12.0, 26.0, 60.0)])
//
// A segment is a solid, so the body is too: min it into the scene. Holes into
// its ends come from mechanical/drill.
//
// THE JOINTS. Segments that meet at a plane cannot simply be min'd: on the
// joint plane, inside both, each capped segment reads 0, so their min is 0
// there too: a sheet of zero inside the solid with no change of sign across
// it. Nothing on the outside shows it, but it breaks every interior value and
// every proof that relies on one, and an inset or a shell opens it into a
// slit. Extending the smaller segment into the larger would close it, but
// that needs to know which side is smaller, and a gear whose tip stands above
// the next diameter while its root lies below it is the smaller side at some
// angles and the larger at others.
//
// So the body also takes a BRIDGE for every RUN of consecutive segments: the
// intersection of all their sections over the run's whole span, the first
// unbounded above, the last unbounded below and those between unbounded both
// ways. Over each member's own span the intersection lies inside that member,
// so it adds no material whatever the convexity. On a joint plane, wherever
// both sides are solid, the run of the two is negative, so the sheet is gone;
// where only one side is solid it is positive and that segment's own end face
// is a real face.
//
// Neighbours alone are not enough. A thin segment, a collar or a ring groove,
// bridged only to each neighbour leaves every point of the section across it
// no deeper than half its width, a shallow sheet in place of a zero one, which
// an inset just as deep still opens. The run of all three carries the section
// common to them straight across it.
//
// The distance is exact outside wherever the segments are: from within any
// member's span, the nearest point of that member extended is the member's own
// (an extension is only ever reached at its rim), so no bridge undercuts the
// min of the capped segments. Inside it is a bound.
//
// A SEGMENT is a factory returning a closure, which only body calls, of two
// extension lengths and a question: seg(0, 0, 0) is the segment capped to its
// own span, seg(big, 0, 0) is the same section unbounded below it, seg(0, big, 0)
// unbounded above it and seg(big, big, 0) both ways; seg(0, 0, 1) is the plane it
// starts on and seg(0, 0, 2) the plane it ends on, which is how body finds the
// shaft's two end faces without being told. Beyond its span a segment continues
// at the diameter it ends with. seg(0, 0, 3) is its cut: nothing, unless an end
// is a cutter runout.
//
// RUNOUTS. A hob or a disc cutter cannot stop at a gear's or a spline's end
// plane. Named with shaft.runout(cutterDia), that end's gaps run on into the
// neighbour, their floor rising from the root along the cutter's arc until it
// breaks out of the neighbour's diameter, and within the cutter's reach the
// neighbour is cut to the segment's profile. body cuts the runouts after the
// fold, as it cuts the end holes. A chamfered end, shaft.chamferEnd(size), cuts
// the segment's own prism at 45 degrees.
//
// THE ENDS. body(segments, bottomHole, topHole) cuts a mechanical/drill hole
// stack into each end face, each depth measured from its own face as a drawing
// gives it. The top stack is cut downward from the top face as drill.hole cuts
// any hole; the bottom one is the same kind of stack turned over by a half turn
// about X, which keeps x and the hand of a thread, so a keyway drawn on +x stays
// on +x.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

// =========================================================
// Entry Points
// =========================================================

/// The solid of a shaft: `segments`, a list of segments from this package
/// (shaft.diameter, shaft.taper), each between its own two planes, in order
/// along +Z, each starting where the one before it ends. A lone segment needs
/// no list. Exact outside, a bound inside, and solid across every joint.
body(segments) {
  // This body is CLONED INTO THE CALLER'S FILE, where the segments' own
  // arguments live, so every name in it has to resolve there: builtin is
  // dot-imported everywhere, a name of this package's would not be. The
  // extension length is written out for the same reason.
  //
  // The accumulator is [the shaft so far, every run still open]. The open
  // runs are the runs that end at the previous segment, each the intersection
  // of its members with the last of them still unbounded above, pooled by min:
  // the bridges of all runs ending here are then max(open, this unbounded
  // below), since max distributes over min. Passing this segment, every open
  // run takes it as a member between (unbounded both ways) and a new run opens
  // at it (unbounded above). The seed's second component keeps the first
  // segment from bridging with anything.
  shaftSoFar = reduce((acc, seg) {
    openRuns = acc.y;
    return [min(acc.x, seg(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), max(openRuns, seg(100000.0, 0.0, 0.0))),
            min(max(openRuns, seg(100000.0, 100000.0, 0.0)), seg(0.0, 100000.0, 0.0))];
  }, segments, [100000.0, 100000.0]);
  // The runouts, cut after the fold: where a cutter runs out past a segment's
  // end, its gaps run on into the neighbour.
  cuts = reduce((c, seg) { return min(c, seg(0.0, 0.0, 3.0)); }, segments, 100000.0);
  return max(shaftSoFar.x, -cuts);
}

/// The same shaft with a hole stack cut into each end face: `bottomHole` into
/// the lowest segment's starting plane and `topHole` into the highest
/// segment's ending plane, each a list of mechanical/drill features, or a lone
/// one, with every depth measured from its own end face. The bottom stack is
/// turned over by a half turn about X, so a feature drawn on +x stays on +x.
///
///   shaft.body(segments, drill.centerHole(3.15, 6.7, 8.0),
///              [drill.tap(6.0, 12.0), drill.blind(5.0, 15.0)])
body(segments, bottomHole, topHole) {
  // Cloned into the caller's file like body(segments), and written out in full
  // for the same reason: it cannot call that one by name.
  shaftSoFar = reduce((acc, seg) {
    openRuns = acc.y;
    return [min(acc.x, seg(0.0, 0.0, 0.0), max(openRuns, seg(100000.0, 0.0, 0.0))),
            min(max(openRuns, seg(100000.0, 100000.0, 0.0)), seg(0.0, 100000.0, 0.0))];
  }, segments, [100000.0, 100000.0]);
  cuts = reduce((c, seg) { return min(c, seg(0.0, 0.0, 3.0)); }, segments, 100000.0);
  // The end faces, asked of the segments: the lowest starting plane and the
  // highest ending one.
  bottomFace = reduce((low, seg) { return min(low, seg(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)); }, segments, 100000.0);
  topFace = reduce((high, seg) { return max(high, seg(0.0, 0.0, 2.0)); }, segments, -100000.0);
  // The voids run on past their faces, as every drill void does, so no surface
  // of either lies in the face it opens onto.
  topVoid = drill.hole(topHole, topFace);
  bottomVoid = remap(drill.hole(bottomHole, 0.0), [p.x, -p.y, bottomFace - p.z]);
  return max(shaftSoFar.x, -cuts, -topVoid, -bottomVoid);
}

// =========================================================
// Segments
// =========================================================

/// A plain diameter `dia` across, from the plane z = `from` up to z = `to`.
/// A groove, a relief or a journal is a diameter too: list it between its
/// neighbours.
diameter(dia, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    radial = length(p.xy) - dia / 2.0;
    axial = max((from - eDown) - p.z, p.z - (to + eUp));
    field = sqrt(max(radial, 0.0) * max(radial, 0.0) + max(axial, 0.0) * max(axial, 0.0)) + min(max(radial, axial), 0.0);
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : segmentAnswer(want, field, from, to);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// A taper from diameter `diaFrom` at the plane z = `from` to diameter
/// `diaTo` at z = `to`: narrowing or widening, a cone shoulder between two
/// diameters, a chamfer at a shaft's end.
taper(diaFrom, diaTo, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : segmentAnswer(want, turnedTaper(diaFrom / 2.0, diaTo / 2.0, from, to, from - eDown, to + eUp), from, to);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// Any 2D section `field`, a distance in the XY plane negative inside such as
/// a profileXY, carried along the axis from z = `from` to z = `to`. Exact
/// wherever the section is: the segment is the section's prism, square-ended.
section(field, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return prismSegment(field, from, to, eDown, eUp, want);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// A hexagon `acrossFlats` wide, its flats facing +x and -x as
/// drill.hexSocket's do. Exact.
hex(acrossFlats, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : prismSegment(hexSection(acrossFlats), from, to, eDown, eUp, want);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// A D-shaft: a round of diameter `dia` with one flat facing +x, `acrossFlat`
/// measured from the flat to the far side of the round, as drill.dFlat's
/// bore is. Exact.
dFlat(dia, acrossFlat, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : prismSegment(dFlatSection(dia, acrossFlat), from, to, eDown, eUp, want);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// A double-D: a round of diameter `dia` with two opposed flats facing +x and
/// -x, `acrossFlats` apart, as drill.doubleD's bore is. Exact.
doubleD(dia, acrossFlats, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : prismSegment(doubleDSection(dia, acrossFlats), from, to, eDown, eUp, want);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// A straight-sided male spline, the DIN 5463 / ISO 14 form: `count` teeth,
/// each `width` across with parallel flanks, standing from the `minor`
/// diameter out to the `major`, one centred on +x. It seats in
/// drill.spline(count, major, width) over a bore at `minor`. Exact outside.
spline(count, minor, major, width, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : prismSegment(splineSection(count, minor, major, width), from, to, eDown, eUp, want);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// The same spline with its two ends named, `bottomEnd` at `from` and
/// `topEnd` at `to`, each shaft.planeEnd(), shaft.runout(cutterDia) or
/// shaft.chamferEnd(size).
spline(count, minor, major, width, from, to, bottomEnd, topEnd) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 && !(bottomEnd.x > 0.5 && bottomEnd.x < 1.5) && !(topEnd.x > 0.5 && topEnd.x < 1.5) ? nothing : machinedSegment(splineSection(count, minor, major, width), major / 2.0, minor / 2.0,
                           from, to, eDown, eUp, want, bottomEnd, topEnd);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// An involute gear cut on the shaft: `numTeeth` teeth of module `moduleVal`
/// and pressure angle `pressureAngle`, the section gears.gearProfile draws
/// (a quarter-module root clearance, a twentieth-module tip break), from
/// z = `from` to z = `to`. A `helixAngle` other than 0 makes it helical,
/// measured at the pitch circle and turning with z from the tooth position at
/// `from`. A bound: the involute section is not exact, and a helix's twist
/// stretches the field, which is divided out so that it never overstates the
/// distance.
gear(numTeeth, moduleVal, pressureAngle, helixAngle, from, to) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 ? nothing : prismSegment(gearSection(numTeeth, moduleVal, pressureAngle, helixAngle, from), from, to, eDown, eUp, want);
  }
  return seg;
}

/// The same gear with its two ends named, `bottomEnd` at `from` and `topEnd`
/// at `to`, each shaft.planeEnd(), shaft.runout(hobDia) or
/// shaft.chamferEnd(size).
///
///   shaft.gear(20.0, 2.0, radians(20.0), 0.0, 20.0, 50.0, shaft.planeEnd(), shaft.runout(80.0))
gear(numTeeth, moduleVal, pressureAngle, helixAngle, from, to, bottomEnd, topEnd) {
  seg(eDown, eUp, want) {
    return want > 2.5 && !(bottomEnd.x > 0.5 && bottomEnd.x < 1.5) && !(topEnd.x > 0.5 && topEnd.x < 1.5) ? nothing : machinedSegment(gearSection(numTeeth, moduleVal, pressureAngle, helixAngle, from),
                           numTeeth * moduleVal / 2.0 + moduleVal, numTeeth * moduleVal / 2.0 - 1.25 * moduleVal,
                           from, to, eDown, eUp, want, bottomEnd, topEnd);
  }
  return seg;
}

// =========================================================
// Ends
// =========================================================

/// A machined segment's end at its plane: the teeth, flats or gaps stop there.
planeEnd() {
  return [0.0, 0.0];
}

/// A machined segment's end where the cutter, of diameter `cutterDia`, runs
/// out: a hob or a disc cutter cannot stop at the plane, so past it the gaps
/// run on into the neighbour, their floor rising from the root along the
/// cutter's arc until it breaks out of the neighbour's diameter.
runout(cutterDia) {
  return [1.0, cutterDia / 2.0];
}

/// A machined segment's end with its blank chamfered by `size` at 45 degrees
/// before the teeth are cut: the tips fall away toward the end plane.
chamferEnd(size) {
  return [2.0, size];
}

// =========================================================
// Internal Geometry
// =========================================================

// A 2D section's prism from z = from to z = to, its ends moved out by the
// extension lengths, answering as segmentAnswer says. The prism of an exact
// section is exact, inside and out.
prismSegment(sectionField, from, to, eDown, eUp, want) {
  axial = max((from - eDown) - p.z, p.z - (to + eUp));
  field = sqrt(max(sectionField, 0.0) * max(sectionField, 0.0) + max(axial, 0.0) * max(axial, 0.0)) + min(max(sectionField, axial), 0.0);
  return want > 2.5 ? nothing : segmentAnswer(want, field, from, to);
}

// The hexagon's six corners, counterclockwise from 30 degrees, so its flats
// face +-x.
hexSection(acrossFlats) {
  r = acrossFlats / 1.7320508075688772;
  cx = r * 0.8660254037844386;
  cy = r * 0.5;
  return profileXY([[cx, cy], [0.0, r], [neg(cx), cy], [neg(cx), neg(cy)], [0.0, neg(r)], [cx, neg(cy)]]);
}

// The D: round from the top of the flat, through -x, to its bottom, in two
// minor arcs, and back up the flat.
dFlatSection(dia, acrossFlat) {
  r = dia / 2.0;
  flatX = acrossFlat - r;
  flatY = sqrt(r * r - flatX * flatX);
  return profileXY([[flatX, flatY], [neg(r), 0.0], [flatX, neg(flatY)]], [1.0 / r, 1.0 / r, 0.0]);
}

// The double D: up the +x flat, over the top arc, down the -x flat and under
// the bottom arc.
doubleDSection(dia, acrossFlats) {
  r = dia / 2.0;
  flatX = acrossFlats / 2.0;
  flatY = sqrt(r * r - flatX * flatX);
  return profileXY([[flatX, neg(flatY)], [flatX, flatY], [neg(flatX), flatY], [neg(flatX), neg(flatY)]],
                   [0.0, 1.0 / r, 0.0, 1.0 / r]);
}

// The spline: one tooth on +x, a box from the axis out to an arc on the major
// circle, repeated round the axis and joined to the minor disc. Every point is
// nearest the tooth of its own sector, so the repeat is exact; tooth and disc
// overlap rather than abut, so their min is exact outside with no sheet inside.
splineSection(count, minor, major, width) {
  halfWidth = width / 2.0;
  majorR = major / 2.0;
  tipX = sqrt(majorR * majorR - halfWidth * halfWidth);
  tooth = remap(profileXY([[0.0, neg(halfWidth)], [tipX, neg(halfWidth)], [tipX, halfWidth], [0.0, halfWidth]],
                          [0.0, 1.0 / majorR, 0.0, 0.0]),
                map.patternPolarXY(count));
  return min(length(p.xy) - minor / 2.0, tooth);
}

// The gear's section at this height: gears.gearProfile turned by the helix's
// twist since `from`, and divided by the most the twist can stretch it, at the
// tip circle, so the field stays a bound.
gearSection(numTeeth, moduleVal, pressureAngle, helixAngle, from) {
  pitchRadius = numTeeth * moduleVal / 2.0;
  tipRadius = pitchRadius + moduleVal;
  twistRate = tan(helixAngle) / pitchRadius;
  twist = (p.z - from) * twistRate;
  cosT = cos(twist);
  sinT = sin(twist);
  profile = remap(gears.gearProfile(numTeeth, moduleVal, pressureAngle, 0.0, 0.25, 0.05 * moduleVal),
                  [p.x * cosT + p.y * sinT, p.y * cosT - p.x * sinT, p.z]);
  return profile / sqrt(1.0 + tipRadius * twistRate * tipRadius * twistRate);
}

// A machined segment whose ends are named. In the fold it is its finished
// prism, capped and extended, as Phase 3's segments are, cut back at 45
// degrees by `size` at a chamferEnd (the blank chamfered before the teeth).
// Its cut, asked only when an end is a runout, is the space outside
// `sectionField` within the cutter's reach past that end: the cutter's disc
// in the half-section, of radius Rc centred at (rootR + Rc, the end plane),
// so the floor of the gaps rises from the root along the cutter's arc into
// the neighbour, which body cuts after the fold. An end is [kind, size]: 0 a
// plane, 1 a runout (size the cutter's radius), 2 a chamfer. The ternaries
// choose only between constants.
machinedSegment(sectionField, blankR, rootR, from, to, eDown, eUp, want, bottomEnd, topEnd) {
  r = length(p.xy);
  radial = r - blankR;
  axial = max((from - eDown) - p.z, p.z - (to + eUp));
  chamferBottom = min((radial + (from - p.z) + bottomEnd.y) / sqrt2, bottomEnd.x > 1.5 ? nothing : neg(nothing));
  chamferTop = min((radial + (p.z - to) + topEnd.y) / sqrt2, topEnd.x > 1.5 ? nothing : neg(nothing));
  prism = sqrt(max(sectionField, 0.0) * max(sectionField, 0.0) + max(axial, 0.0) * max(axial, 0.0)) + min(max(sectionField, axial), 0.0);
  ownField = max(prism, chamferBottom, chamferTop);
  runoutBottom = bottomEnd.x > 0.5 && bottomEnd.x < 1.5;
  runoutTop = topEnd.x > 0.5 && topEnd.x < 1.5;
  reachBottom = max(length([r - (rootR + bottomEnd.y), p.z - from]) - bottomEnd.y, runoutBottom ? neg(nothing) : nothing);
  reachTop = max(length([r - (rootR + topEnd.y), p.z - to]) - topEnd.y, runoutTop ? neg(nothing) : nothing);
  cut = max(neg(sectionField), min(reachBottom, reachTop));
  return want > 2.5 ? (runoutBottom || runoutTop ? cut : nothing) : segmentAnswer(want, ownField, from, to);
}

// What a segment answers: its field when `want` is 0, the plane it starts on
// when 1, the plane it ends on when 2 and its cut when 3, which is nothing
// unless an end runs out. body always asks with a constant, so
// each answer is one of the four outright.
segmentAnswer(want, field, from, to) {
  return want > 2.5 ? nothing : (want > 1.5 ? to : (want > 0.5 ? from : field));
}

// The distance from q to the segment from a to b, in 2D. A segment of length
// zero is its point: an extension of zero leaves one, and it must read as the
// point rather than as nothing.
segmentDistance2(q, a, b) {
  edge = b - a;
  offset = q - a;
  along = clamp(dot(offset, edge) / max(dot(edge, edge), 1e-12), 0.0, 1.0);
  return length(offset - edge * along);
}

// The exact signed distance to a taper of radius r0 at z = from and r1 at
// z = to, continued as plain diameters down to the plane z = low and up to
// z = high (low <= from < to <= high). A prism's formula is not enough here:
// above the small end of a narrowing taper a point can lie nearest the sloped
// side, not the rim, so the distance is taken in the taper's own half-section,
// at q = (distance from the axis, z), as the nearest of its five edges: the
// bottom cap, the side below the taper, the slope, the side above it and the
// top cap. The axis is no edge of it, and for q on its side of the axis the
// nearest point of each cap is too.
//
// The four edges that run along or across the axis are measured by clamping
// rather than by projecting onto them. An extended side runs from a plane a
// hundred metres off, and q minus that end, in float32, keeps only about a
// hundredth of a millimetre; clamping z between the side's two planes only
// ever subtracts the near one. The slope's two ends are both its own, so it
// takes the general segment distance.
//
// The sign comes from containment, low <= z <= high and r <= radius(z), and is
// applied by a select between the magnitude and its negation, as profileXY's
// is: over a surface cell the condition is undecided and the bound widens to
// the magnitude's range, which is the cell's size there.
turnedTaper(r0, r1, from, to, low, high) {
  q = [length(p.xy), p.z];
  bottomCap = length([max(q.x - r0, 0.0), p.z - low]);
  sideBelow = length([q.x - r0, max(low - p.z, p.z - from, 0.0)]);
  slope = segmentDistance2(q, [r0, from], [r1, to]);
  sideAbove = length([q.x - r1, max(to - p.z, p.z - high, 0.0)]);
  topCap = length([max(q.x - r1, 0.0), p.z - high]);
  nearest = min(bottomCap, sideBelow, slope, sideAbove, topCap);
  radiusHere = r0 + (r1 - r0) * clamp((p.z - from) / (to - from), 0.0, 1.0);
  containment = max(low - p.z, p.z - high, q.x - radiusHere);
  return containment > 0.0 ? nearest : neg(nearest);
}
